{
  "rationale": "Constraints first: throughput >= 1 MSPS is non-binding for iterative (N+3 cycles/result, so even N=30 needs only ~33 MHz of Fmax), and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 needs roughly W >= 11-12 with rounding plus N >= ~W-2 for CORDIC convergence. So the binding axis is accuracy vs area, not speed. Fleet data says the whole merged front (13/13 points) is iterative, best feasible luts_plus_ffs 260; unrolled_k's best is 374 with 0 front points and pipelined_m's best is 792 with 0 front points. Since all families compute identical bits for the same (W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding), unrolled_k is pointwise dominated by iterative (same accuracy, k chained micro-rotations per cycle = more add/shift datapath), so it deserves only a small confirmation share. The HV reference point is (1500, 10): the best feasible area already found (260) is far below 1500, so the area axis is slack and the dominant hypervolume lever is the TOP of the accuracy range, i.e. the last front point's (1500 - x)*(accuracy_bits - 10) term. That means the search must reach the high-W / high-N corner (accuracy_bits ~ W-1), not just re-polish the 260-area corner. Hence data_width is opened all the way to the registry max 28 with n_iter tracking it to 30, while still covering the low-area corner (W 10-13) where the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) will pick from.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Holds 13/13 merged-front points and the best feasible area (260). Ranges are opened to the full registry width so the front is mapped from the min-area corner (W~11-12, N~10-14, small guards, rounding=round) up to the high-accuracy corner (W~24-28, N~24-30) that the (1500, 10) reference point rewards most. angle_guard and frac_guard are left free because they are cheap accuracy levers that can beat spending a whole extra bit of W; rounding is left free because round is expected to buy ~1 bit of accuracy at fixed W for near-zero area.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 10, "high": 28, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 4, "high": 30, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
