{
  "rationale": "The score is hypervolume, and the current HV is almost entirely set by the top-accuracy corner: reconstructing the staircase against ref (1500, 10) from the reported front, the last segment (area 580 -> 1500 at 20.8 bits) contributes ~9.9e3 of the 1.172e4 total (~85%), while every low-area segment together contributes <1.7e3. So the highest-leverage move is to push accuracy_bits above 20.8, not to shave LUTs at the bottom. Evidence that this is reachable: the top design uses n_iter=22 although the registry allows 30, and angle_guard=0 although 4 is allowed; on the front, raising angle_guard 0->4 at data_width=22/n_iter=22 bought +1.2 bits (16.9 -> 18.1), and raising n_iter 14->22 at data_width=17 bought +2.8 bits (11.4 -> 14.2). The top design is data_width=28 (registry max), so the remaining headroom is in n_iter and the guard bits. The low-area end (278 LUT+FF, 11.3 bits) already clears the 2^-10 constraint with ~1.3 bits of slack and adds almost no HV, and the reserved end-of-run mapping round will sweep the full ranges anyway. Throughput is a non-issue (best 36.5 MSPS, smallest front design 6.13 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS floor), so pipelined/pipelined_m would only add FFs for identical accuracy and are dominated on the area objective; no need to add a family.",
